WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.920
Captain Marvel is the introduction of the
the most powerful hero in the MCU to date.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:09.880
It involves a complex web of characters new
and old, and it's also a semi-prequel set

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.660
in the '90s, which means there are a lot of
questions left largely unexplored.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.300
Be warned, MAJOR SPOILERS AHEAD.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.500
Captain Marvel gives us yet another wrinkle
in the history of the Tesseract, the first

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.360
Infinity Stone to make its way to Earth.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:30.410
We knew that the Tesseract, a.k.a. the Space
Stone, was found by HYDRA in the 1940s, recovered

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:35.700
by SHIELD after Captain America's disappearance,
and then stolen by Loki in the 2010s.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.660
Now, we know that Dr. Wendy Lawson, a.k.a.
Mar-Vell, was a gifted Kree scientist who

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:46.929
used the Tesseract as part of her work on
Project Pegasus, using it to power a lightspeed

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:49.940
engine before her death sometime around the
late '80s.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.219
In the six years between Mar-Vell's death
and the rise of Carol Danvers as a superhero,

00:00:55.219 --> 00:01:00.170
the Tesseract apparently just sat hidden in
her cloaked, orbiting lab, which raises a

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.340
few questions.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.920
She was presumably allowed the use of it by
S.H.I.E.L.D., so why didn't SHIELD come looking

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:06.980
for it when she died?

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.540
How could something so important go missing
for so long?

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.740
One of the standout stars of Captain Marvel
is Goose, Mar-Vell's quote unquote cat.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.300
By the end of the film, it's revealed that
Goose is a Flerken, an alien species that

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.210
looks like an Earth feline on the outside,
but also packs deadly tentacles and the ability

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.920
to consume objects several times its size.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.630
During the film's climactic moments, Goose
devours several Kree warriors, intimidates

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.620
other aliens, and even eats the Tesseract
so Fury won't have to touch it.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.920
Because we know what happens when the wrong
people touch Infinity Stones.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.980
Then, in the post-credits scene, we see Goose
hwark the Infinity Stone up onto Fury's desk

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.280
and back into S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:52.780
custody.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.440
Is Goose still hanging around the MCU somewhere?

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.860
We may never know, but Goose and Fury seem
like good pals.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.060
"Aren't you the cutest thing ever what’s
your name?”

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:04.660
“Fury”

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:07.120
“I’ll be back."

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:13.459
The legendary Nick Fury still has both of
his eyes for most of Captain Marvel, and he's

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:16.120
also still a rather green as a lowly Level
3 S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:16.620
agent.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.680
We know that clearance Levels within S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:24.040
go up to 10, with a couple of even more secretive
levels above that, which is where Fury eventually

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:24.560
ends up.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.000
The film ends around 1995, which gives him
a little more than a decade to become director

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.640
by the time he meets Tony Stark in 2008.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.231
We know he gets the director job after forming
a relationship with Alexander Pierce, who

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.760
leaves the position to become Secretary of
the World Security Council.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.960
What we don't know is exactly how long that
took.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.680
Fury's rapid rise through the ranks from a
wimpy Level 3 agent had to be meteoric, right?

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.680
"That's a yes or no question."

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:52.620
"Yes."

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.340
Among the film's many familiar faces is Ronan
the Accuser, Kree dispatcher of justice and

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.450
the main villain in Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.140
There, Ronan is a rogue Accuser who becomes
a terrorist fanatic after his people sign

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.620
a peace treaty with Xandar.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:12.120
But 20 years earlier, he's still obedient
to the Kree government, working in close collaboration

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.460
with Yon-Rogg and the rest of the Starforce.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.660
We know that Ronan eventually goes rogue,
defies his people, and ultimately dies in

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.160
a struggle over the Power Stone.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.480
Which is not an uncommon theme for Infinity
Stones.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.360
At the end of Captain Marvel, though, he says
that he'll one day return for Carol, a human

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:33.860
weapon.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.000
So, does he ever return for her?

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.340
She leaves Earth at the end of the film, but
did he and the other Accusers ever try to

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:41.700
seek her out in space?

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:46.400
We don't know right now, but a rivalry between
Captain Marvel and a Kree Accuser could be

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:46.900
interesting.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.180
Another familiar face is Korath the Pursuer.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:51.820
"Who?"

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:56.060
By the end of his life, he's one of Ronan's
top lieutenants, but his eventual defection

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:00.739
to follow Ronan is hinted at only briefly,
in a scene in which he seems more willing

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.180
than Yon-Rogg to tell the Accuser about Carol's
whereabouts.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.110
The last time we see Korath in the film, he's
battling Carol, only to be defeated along

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.600
with the rest of the Starforce.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.120
Korath's state at the end of Captain Marvel
isn't exactly clear, but we know that when

00:04:15.139 --> 00:04:19.919
he returns in Guardians of the Galaxy, he's
working with Ronan, and he has several enhancements,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.020
including equipment grafted into his head.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.440
So, what happened?

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.720
Did Ronan rebuild Korath and give him his
enhancements?

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.900
What turns one Kree against another?

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:30.580
Probably Thanos.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:31.819
It's always Thanos.

00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:37.669
One of the most exciting pieces potential
story in Captain Marvel is the Rambeaus: Carol's

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:42.569
best friend Maria and her daughter Monica,
who idolizes "Auntie Carol" when she's a pilot,

00:04:42.569 --> 00:04:45.340
even more so when it becomes clear that she's
a superhero.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.660
In Marvel's comics, we know that Monica Rambeau
actually held the Captain Marvel title in

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.240
1982, long before Carol did.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.660
She's since shifted superhero identities several
times and is currently known as Spectrum.

00:04:59.009 --> 00:05:03.580
By any name, she's a vital hero in the Marvel
Universe who once led the Avengers and can

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.480
summon tremendous power of her own.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:10.580
She'd be in her 30s by the time the present
day MCU events happen, so will we see her

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:11.880
show up as a hero?

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.000
Skrulls are an insidious force that will infiltrate
any planet using their shapeshifting abilities,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.360
and ultimately take it over.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:25.340
The Supreme Intelligence tells us this, Yon-Rogg
tells this, and even Carol tells us this.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.520
Except that's a big, fat lie.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:32.620
The remaining Skrulls in the galaxy are actually
refugees, running from the Kree as they look

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.700
for a new home; something Mar-Vell was hoping
to solve with her lightspeed engine.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.120
With Carol's help, they eventually head off
into the stars to find a new planet to settle

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.340
on, and that's the last we hear of them.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:45.620
Do the Skrulls find a planet to call home?

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.120
If they do, then they've had more than two
decades to colonize and rebuild since the

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.520
events of Captain Marvel.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.140
So will they eventually become an expanding
power in the galaxy again?

00:05:55.610 --> 00:06:00.200
The resilience of the Skrulls, and their ability
to rise up and conquer again and again even

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.380
after their defeat is one of their comic book
trademarks.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.140
Sometimes, they're humanity's enemy, and sometimes
they're an ally.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:10.560
So where are the Skrulls now, and whose side
are they on?

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.020
And will they be the ones to come back the
and help kick some Thanos butt?

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:19.000
When Carol finally remembers how she got her
powers and ultimately arrived at the Kree

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.460
homeworld, everything she knows is flipped
upside-down.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.690
Instead of being an ally, Yon-Rogg and his
pals are the ones who shot down Danvers' and

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:30.000
Mar-Vell's lightspeed ship and killed Mar-Vell.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.580
When Carol destroys the engine and absorbs
its power, Yon-Rogg abducts her, and hides

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:37.580
her memories from her for six years.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:42.180
After Carol finds out, she defeats her former
mentor and sends him back to Hala, where he'll

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:43.689
have to face retribution.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:45.500
So, what happens to Yon-Rogg?

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:47.240
Is it a light reprimand?

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:47.740
Prison?

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:48.880
Death by Flerken?

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:50.460
Will we ever see him again?

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.460
In a universe this complex, anyone left alive
is potentially fodder for sequels.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.260
Carol Danvers receives her powers when she
destroys Mar-Vell's lightspeed engine, granting

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:06.280
her superhuman strength, speed, resilience,
and the photon blasts that become her defining

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:06.780
power.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:12.550
But that explosive engine was powered by the
Tesseract, aka the Space Stone, an object

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:16.820
of tremendous power that's played numerous
major roles in the Marvel Universe.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.150
The Space Stone can transport its bearer across
great distances, as it did with the Red Skull

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:24.760
in The First Avenger, and Thanos in Infinity
War.

00:07:25.169 --> 00:07:30.340
We also know that it can possibly be triggered
from afar, as Loki did in The Avengers.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:34.430
Carol Danvers gets her power from it, just
as Vision derived some of his abilities from

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:36.460
the Mind Stone embedded in his head.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.120
Does that mean she can manipulate the Space
Stone or use some fraction of its abilities?

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:44.520
Is she as intimately connected to it as Vision
is to the Mind Stone?

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:45.780
Can she teleport?

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:50.740
These are enormous questions, and Avengers:
Endgame might ultimately provide us with some

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:51.280
answers.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.180
We all know you have to be careful with those
Stones, though.

